<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#func-healing> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "This element — sign, rhythm, plant use, or artifact — is employed as part of a healing or cleansing ceremony, calling protective or restorative forces."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#AssertionStatusScheme> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "Controlled terms for whether a claim is widely attested, lineage-specific, or actively contested."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#PublicTitleTypeScheme> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "Classification of dc:title values indicating whether a title represents the entity's own name or a surrogate assigned by custodians, administrators, or institutions."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#sacredStatus-contested-consecration> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#region-central-african> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Traditions with primary roots in the Bantu and Equatorial communities of Central Africa, including the Kongo kingdom and its successor traditions, Luba, Mbundu, Chokwe, and related cultures of the Congo River basin and its watersheds."@en .
